{
    "title": "Safety Zone; San Francisco New Year's Eve Fireworks; San Francisco, CA",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 17, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-17/pdf/2025-23125.pdf",
    "summary": "Big picture: this notice bundles three federal updates\u2014stronger emergency planning and formula purchasing rules for the Special Supplemental Nutrition Program for Women, Infants, and Children; a temporary boating safety zone for San Francisco\u2019s New Year\u2019s Eve fireworks; and a correction to Medicare payment rates for inpatient rehabilitation facilities.\n\nFor the nutrition program, the Department of Agriculture is tightening how states plan for emergencies and recalls and how they buy infant formula. States must keep a clear communications plan to inform families, vendors, and the public during crises. They also have to use a consistent method to compare bids for different types of formula so they can choose the best overall price, not just the biggest rebate. If a state wants to pick a bid based on the highest rebate, it first has to show that retail prices for competing brands in that state are nearly the same. Importantly, states may issue non-contract infant formula without a doctor\u2019s note\u2014except for special medical packages\u2014giving families more flexibility when supplies are tight. The rule also clarifies what states must include when asking for waivers during emergencies.\n\nFamilies who rely on the program could see more dependable access to formula during shortages and clearer updates when recalls happen. Taxpayers and state agencies may benefit from more apples-to-apples bidding and potentially better pricing. On the flip side, states will have new administrative work to gather price data and run standardized bid comparisons. Manufacturers that rely on exclusive contracts might see less certainty if states can more easily issue other brands during disruptions, and some vendors may need to adjust stocking and pricing strategies.\n\nThe Coast Guard is enforcing a safety zone around the fireworks barge near the San Francisco Ferry Building from noon on December 31 through 12:45 a.m. on January 1. This helps protect people, boats, and the environment during loading, transit, and the show. Spectators and event crews benefit from a safer waterfront, while recreational boaters\u2014and possibly some commercial vessels\u2014will face short, clearly defined restrictions on movement.\n\nFinally, the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services corrected a technical error in its previously finalized payment rates for inpatient rehabilitation facilities. One provider was mistakenly left out of the data because of a numbering issue, which slightly changes the outlier threshold and some estimated payment impacts. Patients and facilities benefit from more accurate payments that reflect the full data set, though some providers may experience small, unexpected adjustments after budgets were set. The agency emphasized that this is a correction, not a policy change.",
